---
/**
 * /mission — the problem, the principles, and the plan.
 *
 * Rewritten to integrate the stronger framing from the unified vision
 * doc: the "What's Broken" problem statement, the design principles,
 * and tighter "where we're going" language. Contribution details live
 * on /contribute — this page is about vision and values.
 */

import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { stats } from "../lib/concepts.ts";
import { allTopics } from "../lib/topics.ts";
import { wishlistStats } from "../lib/wishlist.ts";
import { graveyardStats } from "../lib/negative-results.ts";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Mission — So Far So Good"
  description="Science doesn't have a knowledge problem. It has an infrastructure problem. We're building the connective tissue."
  nav="mission"
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Header                                                      */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <header class="mb-20 hero-glow">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-3">The mission</div>
      <h1
        class="font-display text-4xl md:text-6xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.05]"
      >
        Science doesn't have a knowledge problem. It has an infrastructure problem.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-8 text-lg leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--color-ink-dim);">
        The data exists. The talent exists. The questions exist. But they sit
        in disconnected silos, locked behind paywalls and misaligned incentives.
        This platform is the connective tissue.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* What's broken                                               */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-20">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-4 pb-2 border-b hairline">
        01 &middot; What's broken
      </div>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Modern science runs on a publishing model designed in the 17th century.
        Researchers spend years producing a static PDF, submit it to anonymous
        gatekeepers, wait months for a verdict, and then — whether accepted or
        rejected — the underlying data, code, and failed experiments mostly
        disappear.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-8">
        The result is staggering waste. Over <strong class="text-accent">$28 billion annually</strong> in
        the U.S. alone is estimated lost to irreproducible research. Negative
        results go unpublished. Duplication of effort is rampant. And the
        incentive structure rewards novelty and volume over truth and rigor.
      </p>

      {/* Current vs. Needed table */}
      <div
        class="rounded-lg border overflow-hidden"
        style="border-color: var(--color-paper-edge);"
      >
        <div
          class="grid grid-cols-2 text-[0.65rem] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest px-5 py-3"
          style="background: var(--color-paper-raised); color: var(--color-ink-dim);"
        >
          <span>Current model</span>
          <span>What's needed</span>
        </div>
        {[
          ["Static papers, frozen at publication", "Living documents, continuously updated"],
          ["Data buried in supplements or lost", "Data, code, and methods inline and executable"],
          ["Anonymous, one-shot peer review", "Transparent, continuous community evaluation"],
          ["Credit only for first authors on novel findings", "Credit for every contribution — replication, review, curation"],
          ["Knowledge stored as disconnected documents", "A structured, queryable graph of claims and evidence"],
        ].map(([current, needed]) => (
          <div
            class="grid grid-cols-2 px-5 py-3.5 border-t text-sm"
            style="border-color: var(--color-paper-edge);"
          >
            <span style="color: var(--color-ink-faint);">{current}</span>
            <span class="font-medium">{needed}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Design principles                                           */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-20">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-4 pb-2 border-b hairline">
        02 &middot; Design principles
      </div>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-8" style="color: var(--color-ink-dim);">
        These are the non-negotiable commitments that distinguish this from
        another academic tool.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {[
          {
            name: "Truth over attention",
            body: "The system must make careful, verified, replicated work more rewarding than flashy, unreproducible claims. Every incentive mechanism is tested against this standard.",
          },
          {
            name: "Process over product",
            body: "Science is not a paper. It's the ongoing cycle of question, experiment, analysis, revision, and challenge. The platform treats this cycle — not the snapshot — as the first-class object.",
          },
          {
            name: "Access without compromise",
            body: "Free or near-free for researchers everywhere. Multilingual. Lightweight enough for low-bandwidth environments. Independent researchers and small institutions are full citizens, not second-class users.",
          },
          {
            name: "Accountability without gatekeeping",
            body: "Verified identities for credibility. Pseudonymous contributions allowed with reputation tracking. Transparent history. The goal is trust through transparency, not exclusion through credentials.",
          },
          {
            name: "Speed without recklessness",
            body: "Publication in minutes, not months. But every claim carries its evidence, its reproducibility score, its review history. Fast publishing paired with continuous, visible evaluation.",
          },
        ].map((p) => (
          <div
            class="pl-5 border-l-2"
            style="border-color: var(--color-accent);"
          >
            <div class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2 text-accent">
              {p.name}
            </div>
            <p class="text-base leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--color-ink-dim);">
              {p.body}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Where we are                                                */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-20">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-4 pb-2 border-b hairline">
        03 &middot; Where we are now
      </div>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Right now, we index <strong class="text-accent">{stats.totalConcepts} speculative aerospace concepts</strong> from
        NASA's Innovative Advanced Concepts program — fusion engines, solar
        sails, gravitational lensing telescopes, submarine explorers for
        Titan's methane seas. Each one is a real idea that someone got
        funded to seriously study.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        We have <a href="/explore" class="text-accent underline hover:text-white">{allTopics.length} curated deep dives</a> into
        the big scientific questions these concepts touch. A <a href="/wishlist" class="text-accent underline hover:text-white">wishlist
        of {wishlistStats.total} papers</a> spanning {wishlistStats.yearSpan} years.
        A <a href="/graveyard" class="text-accent underline hover:text-white">graveyard
        of {graveyardStats.total} negative results</a> that maps explored territory.
        A <a href="/pulse" class="text-accent underline hover:text-white">live pulse</a> of
        what the world is talking about in space and science.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed">
        It's a start. There's a long way to go.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Where we're going                                           */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-20">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-4 pb-2 border-b hairline">
        04 &middot; Where we're going
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            A structured knowledge graph
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            Every claim on the platform becomes a node. Every piece of evidence
            becomes a node. The edges between them are typed: <em>supports,
            contradicts, replicates, extends, depends on</em>. When you open
            any question, you see not a list of papers but a living map — where
            the evidence clusters, where it conflicts, and where the blank
            spaces are.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            AI-assisted discovery
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            Machine intelligence surfaces what humans miss: connections between
            distant fields, statistical inconsistencies, overlooked datasets,
            and hypothesis generation from structured knowledge. AI proposes,
            humans evaluate. The loop is always human-validated.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            The best explanations, linked to primary sources
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            Science communicators like Veritasium, 3Blue1Brown, PBS Space
            Time, and Quanta Magazine do extraordinary work making hard ideas
            accessible. Their explanations should live alongside the papers and
            data they draw from — so you can go from "that was a great video"
            to "here's the actual paper" in one click.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            History as a first-class citizen
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            You cannot understand the present without understanding the past.
            We want to make those lineages visible — so you can see that the
            concept of a "dark star" predates Einstein by 130 years, that the
            rocket equation Tsiolkovsky wrote in 1903 is still the constraint
            every mission designer fights today.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Contribute                                                  */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-20">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-4 pb-2 border-b hairline">
        05 &middot; How to help
      </div>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        This is an open-source project and we need help. Curate a topic, tag
        a concept, suggest a paper, submit a negative result, recommend a
        video, or write code.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/contribute"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-accent eyebrow hover:text-white transition"
      >
        <span>See concrete actions on the contribute page</span>
        <span>&rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Closing                                                     */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-12 pt-12 border-t hairline">
      <blockquote class="pull-quote max-w-2xl">
        "The best way to predict the future of science is to build its
        infrastructure."
      </blockquote>
      <p class="mt-8 text-base text-dim leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        We believe the next great scientific insight is more likely to happen
        when a curious person can wander freely through all of human
        knowledge — from a 1783 thought experiment about dark stars to a
        2025 NASA study on gravitational lensing — and notice a connection
        that nobody else has seen. That's what we're building toward.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* CTA                                                         */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-8">
      <a
        href="https://github.com/anthonyguzzardo/SoFarSoGood"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-accent eyebrow hover:text-white transition"
      >
        <span>View the project on GitHub</span>
        <span>&rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
